{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794137235,"nonce":"4UzkHB1IsO3ARSfA9rKYsQvQ7P4=","title":"Offline shader pipeline: precompile Blinc's shaders to every backend and drop the runtime compiler","message":"About 16k lines of WGSL across 50 vertex and 62 fragment entry points are compiled at runtime through naga (12.5 s of CPU in a clean build, plus translation time at launch). A native backend needs the shaders already in its own language: MSL for Metal, SPIR-V for Vulkan, HLSL for D3D12, GLSL ES for WebGL2, and WGSL for WebGPU, which browsers require.\n\nPrecompile at build or release time and ship the artifacts, so no compiler runs on the user's machine. Two source options to compare: keep WGSL as the single source and emit the other four with naga's tools, or move to Slang, which targets SPIR-V, MSL, HLSL, GLSL and WGSL and has the pointer and bindless features a root-arguments design needs (WGSL has no buffer pointers). Writing our own Rust-macro shader language would still need all five emitters and a port of the 16k lines; revisit it only if neither option fits.\n\nAlso check wgpu passthrough shader modules, so the wgpu backend can take the precompiled artifacts and naga's frontends can leave the runtime path.\n\nParent: the lean GPU stack plan.","files":null}]}